-- title:
--   Reduction mod p is injective on points: unipotent case
-- statement:
--   Let $O$ be a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring, let $p$ be a prime number such that $(p : O)$ is irreducible, i.e. a uniformiser of $O$, and let $k$ be a field equipped with an $O$-algebra structure whose structure map $O \to k$ is surjective with kernel the ideal generated by $(p : O)$, so that $k$ is the residue field $O/pO$. Let $H$ be a commutative ring which is a Hopf algebra over $O$, cocommutative as an $O$-coalgebra, and finite and free as an $O$-module, and assume there is a natural number $a$ with $\operatorname{finrank}_O H = p^a$. Assume further that [`CartierDual k (TensorProduct O k H)`](def/HopfAlgebra_CartierDual.html#L12), that is the $k$-linear dual $\operatorname{Hom}_k(k \otimes_O H, k)$ of the special fibre with its ring structure, is a local ring. Let $Y$ be a commutative ring which is an $O$-algebra, finite and free as an $O$-module. Then any two $O$-algebra homomorphisms $x, x' : H \to Y$ such that $x(h) - x'(h)$ lies in the ideal generated by $(p : Y)$ for every $h \in H$ are equal.
--
--   In scheme-theoretic language, for a finite flat commutative group scheme $G = \operatorname{Spec} H$ over an absolutely unramified discrete valuation ring $O$ of mixed characteristic $(0,p)$ whose special fibre has local Cartier dual (is unipotent), the reduction map $G(Y) \to G(Y/pY)$ is injective; this is the first step of Fontaine's classification of finite commutative $p$-group schemes by finite Honda systems, in the form valid for all primes including $p = 2$. It is used in the Dieudonné-module and Honda-system parts of the deformation-theoretic input, notably by [`Deformation.DieudonneModule.map_baseChange_injective_and_exists_map_baseChange_eq`](thm.html#Deformation.DieudonneModule.map_baseChange_injective_and_exists_map_baseChange_eq) and [`Deformation.HondaSystem.exists_towerHom_map_comp_eq_comp_of_map_L_le`](thm.html#Deformation.HondaSystem.exists_towerHom_map_comp_eq_comp_of_map_L_le), and the proof proceeds through [`HopfAlgebra.convPow_finrank_eq_one_of_isCocomm`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.convPow_finrank_eq_one_of_isCocomm), the statement that every algebra-homomorphism point has convolution order dividing $\operatorname{finrank}_O H$.
